27. tragedy of the commons [ai]


I feel like we're living the tragedy of the commons
at a planetary scale, both on land and online.
What were the commons, you ask?


Before enclosure,
large portions of English land were "common"
Landowners — often lords of the manor — began fencing off common land
and consolidating it into private holdings.
This happened in waves,
but the most intense period was roughly 1750–1850,
driven by Parliamentary Enclosure Acts.
Parliament passed over 5,000 individual acts
affecting millions of acres.
A commission would arrive in a village,
assess the land, and redistribute it — overwhelmingly in favor of larger landowners.


Smaller holders often received plots too small to be viable,
and the landless poor received nothing at all,
because customary rights weren't recognized as legal property.
It also destroyed centuries of local ecological knowledge
and collective land management.
E.P. Thompson, Karl Polanyi, and others have argued
this was essentially a massive upward transfer of wealth —
what we might today call primitive accumulation —
justified through legal mechanisms
that gave it the appearance of order and legitimacy.


The people dispossessed, rarely had the political power to resist,
and many who tried to were prosecuted under new property laws.


